  "query_preview": "..\n\nRest of Today through Monday...\n\nGusty winds will continue to spread across the area this\nafternoon as a shortwave trough passes through northern Montana. \nThe strongest wind gusts (40s and 50s mph) are occurring\nBillings, especially from Reed Point through Livingston as well \nas US-191 from Big Timber through Judith Gap. A surface pressure \ngradient that is generally oriented in a west- east direction will\ncontinue to favor the US-191 corridor for the strongest gusts \ninto this afternoon. If you have travel plans west of Billings, \nprepare for gusty crosswinds. For non-foothill locations, \nnorthwest winds gusting in the 20s to low 40s (greatest across\nsouth-central Montana) mph will continue into the afternoon.\n\nWinds will diminish across much of the area this evening as the\naforementioned shortwave exits the Northern Rockies. Breezy\nconditions will remain for the Livingston and Nye areas into\nWednesday, with a modest pressure gradient setup over the \nParadise Valley. Expect gusts in the 20s and 30s mph for the\nmentioned areas.\n\nWednesday through Saturday, upper ridging builds over the region\nbringing warm and dry conditions. Highs will range from the upper\n50s to 60s most days, well above normal for this time of year.\nHeading into Sunday and Monday, ensembles and cluster analysis \nshow lower heights building into the region as upper troughing \ngradually pushes inland over the western US, bringing a breakdown \nof the upper ridge. This brings the potential for cooler and more \nunsettled conditions for the week ahead. STP/PM",
